A(('p', 'Mark’s sayings use other words. Mark has “a man taking a far journey,” “servants,” “the porter” and “the master of the house” (13:34–35). Luke 12:36–38 has “men that wait for their lord,” “the wedding,” “the lord” and “servants.” Matthew 24:43 and Luke 12:39 have “the goodman of the house” and “the thief.” The word “thief” stands in Matthew 24:43 and Luke 12:39 and in no verse of Mark 13:33–37 or Luke 12:35–38. The word “porter” stands in Mark 13:34 only. The words “wedding” and “gird himself” stand in Luke 12:36–37 only. Where one account has a word the others lack, the word stays in its own account.'))
A(('p', '3.11 What the accounts contain. Matthew names a goodman of the house, a thief, a watch, a house broken up, a command to be ready, and the Son of man coming at an hour not thought. Mark names a man taking a far journey, his house, his servants, their authority and work, a porter commanded to watch, a master of the house who comes at one of four times, sleeping, and a word to all. Luke names loins girded and lights burning, men waiting for their lord from the wedding, a knocking and an opening, servants found watching, a lord who girds himself and serves, the second and third watch, a goodman and a thief, a house broken through, and the Son of man coming at an hour not thought.'))
A(('p', '3.12 What the accounts leave unsaid. Jesus does not say who the thief is, whose house is meant, or what the watch and the hour are. Jesus does not say who the servants are, who the porter is, or what the work given to every man is. Jesus does not say where the man went on his far journey, or why he went. Jesus does not say what the girded loins and the burning lights are, who the lord is beyond the words of the text, or what the wedding is. Jesus does not say what is meant by the lord girding himself, or serving. Jesus does not say what the four times, or the second and the third watch, are. None of the accounts says that the thief came, that the house was broken, or that anyone was found sleeping. Each stops at the word of application.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives words of application, and no explanation of the parts. None of the three accounts records an explanation of the thief, the house, the servants or the lord, and none records a request for one. What each account records is what Jesus Himself says to the hearers: a likeness in Mark, a likeness in Luke, a blessing, and a word of readiness or of watching. These are His own words and they are recorded here as He said them.'))
A(('tbl', 'Jesus’ own words of application are laid out below, verse by verse.', [
    ['Matt 24:44', '“Therefore be ye also ready: for in such an hour as ye think not the Son of man cometh.”', 'A command to be ready, with the reason given.'],
    ['Mark 13:33', '“Take ye heed, watch and pray: for ye know not when the time is.”', 'A command to watch, with the reason given.'],
    ['Mark 13:34', '“For the Son of man is as a man taking a far journey”', 'Jesus’ likeness. The words that follow describe the man.'],
    ['Mark 13:35', '“Watch ye therefore: for ye know not when the master of the house cometh”', 'A command to watch, with the reason given.'],
    ['Mark 13:36', '“Lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping.”', 'The purpose of the command.'],
    ['Mark 13:37', '“And what I say unto you I say unto all, Watch.”', 'Jesus’ closing word.'],
    ['Luke 12:36', '“And ye yourselves like unto men that wait for their lord”', 'Jesus’ likeness, spoken to “ye.”'],
    ['Luke 12:37', '“Blessed are those servants, whom the lord when he cometh shall find watching”', 'A blessing; the lord’s action follows in the verse.'],
    ['Luke 12:38', '“blessed are those servants”', 'The blessing repeated for the second and third watch.'],
    ['Luke 12:40', '“Be ye therefore ready also: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not.”', 'A command to be ready, with the reason given.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '4.2 What the words say. In Matthew 24:44 and Luke 12:40, Jesus commands readiness and gives as the reason that the Son of man cometh at an hour not thought. In Mark 13:34, Jesus says “the Son of man is as a man taking a far journey,” and the words that follow describe that man. In Mark 13:35–37 He commands watching, gives the reason, “ye know not when the master of the house cometh,” and says “I say unto all, Watch.” In Luke 12:36 He says “ye yourselves” are “like unto men that wait for their lord.” In Luke 12:37–38 He pronounces a blessing on servants found watching. These are the places where Jesus Himself applies the sayings, and they are all that He applies.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What each word does not say. The likeness in Mark 13:34 compares the Son of man to a man taking a far journey. It does not go on to name the servants, the house, the porter or the work. The likeness in Luke 12:36 names “men that wait for their lord.” It does not name the lord, the wedding or the knocking. The words of readiness in Matthew 24:44 and Luke 12:40 give the Son of man’s coming at an hour not thought as the reason. They do not say that the thief is the Son of man, that the goodman is anyone, or that the thief’s coming and the Son of man’s coming are the same event. The words “unto all” in Mark 13:37 do not say who is included in “all.”'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. The sayings name many parts and leave each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the thief. Jesus does not identify the goodman of the house or the house. Jesus does not identify the watch or the hour. Jesus does not identify the servants, the porter or the work given to every man. Jesus does not identify the far journey. Jesus does not identify the master of the house beyond the words of the text. Jesus does not identify the four times of Mark 13:35. Jesus does not identify the lord of Luke 12:36–38, the wedding, the girded loins or the burning lights. Jesus does not identify the second watch or the third watch. Jesus does not identify the lord’s girding himself and serving.'))
