A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification beyond the likenesses of Mark 13:34 and Luke 12:36.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives only words of application, nothing more is given here. A reader who says that the thief is a certain person or event, that the house is a certain people, that the porter or the servants are a certain office, that the lord is named by a title the text does not give in these verses, or that the girded loins and the lights are a certain work or witness is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.5 What the text does say. The text says that if the goodman of the house had known what watch or hour the thief would come, he would have watched and would not have suffered his house to be broken up or broken through; that Jesus says to be ready, because the Son of man cometh at an hour not thought; that the Son of man is as a man taking a far journey who left his house, gave authority to his servants and to every man his work, and commanded the porter to watch; that no one knows when the master of the house cometh, at even, at midnight, at the cockcrowing or in the morning; that Jesus says “Lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping” and “I say unto all, Watch”; that men are to wait for their lord with loins girded and lights burning; that servants found watching are blessed; and that the lord shall gird himself, make them sit down to meat, and come forth and serve them. These statements are made by the sayings and by Jesus’ words of application, and they are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'Jesus gives words of application and no explanation of the parts, so this section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself, set beside those words. It assigns no identity to any part of the sayings and it dates nothing.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. In Matthew and Luke the comparison is a house-keeper who, had he known the hour of a thief, would have watched. In Mark it is a man who goes on a far journey, leaving his servants authority and work and his porter a command to watch, and who comes back at a time not known. In Luke it is men who wait for their lord with loins girded and lights burning, so that they can open at once, and who are blessed when he finds them watching. In each, the meaning is in what the comparison shows of watching and of an hour that is not known, and in the word of application that follows.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Not knowing the hour is the reason for watching.** The goodman would have watched had he known the hour of the thief (Matthew 24:43; Luke 12:39). The hearers are told they do not know: “ye know not when the master of the house cometh” (Mark 13:35); “the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not” (Luke 12:40).',
    '**Readiness is commanded, and the reason is given.** “Therefore be ye also ready” (Matthew 24:44); “Be ye therefore ready also” (Luke 12:40). Readiness is not left to be inferred.',
    '**A charge is given before the absence.** The man “gave authority to his servants, and to every man his work, and commanded the porter to watch” (Mark 13:34). Something is entrusted before he goes, and the porter is told to watch.',
    '**Sleeping at the coming is the danger named.** “Lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping” (Mark 13:36). The coming is described as sudden.',
    '**Being found watching is blessed.** “Blessed are those servants, whom the lord when he cometh shall find watching” (Luke 12:37). The blessing holds if he comes “in the second watch, or come in the third watch” (Luke 12:38).',
    '**Waiting is done in readiness.** Loins girded and lights burning, that the lord on knocking may be opened to “immediately” (Luke 12:35–36).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Matthew names are the disciples (24:3), who had asked about the sign of His coming and had heard that no man knows the day and hour (24:36). The people Mark names are Peter, James, John and Andrew (13:3). The people Luke names are His disciples, with a multitude gathered (12:1, 12:22). They were meant to understand that a coming whose hour is not known calls for watching and readiness, that something is entrusted in the meanwhile, and that being found watching is blessed. Matthew, Mark and Luke do not say that the goodman, the porter, the servants or the men who wait are these hearers, and Jesus does not identify them.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. In Matthew and Luke the force is in the supposition: a keeper who knew the hour would have watched, and the hearers do not know it, so they are told to be ready. In Mark the force is in the contrast between a charge given and a coming that finds sleeping, and in the closing word, “Watch.” In Luke the force is in the blessing, and in the lord who girds himself and serves the servants found watching. That action is reported as written. Luke does not explain it, and nothing is added to it here.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. Watching is called for because the hour is not known. Readiness is commanded. A charge is given before an absence. Being found sleeping at a sudden coming is warned against. Being found watching is blessed. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the thief, the house, the servants, the porter, the lord or the wedding represents anyone or anything. They do not date the coming of the Son of man, and they do not set it in order with any other event.'))

