A(('bul', [
    '**Between characters.** The goodman and the thief; the man on a far journey, his servants and his porter; the men who wait and their lord. Jesus names none of them beyond His two likenesses.',
    '**Between actions.** The goodman would have watched. The man left, gave authority and work, and commanded the porter to watch. The men gird their loins, keep their lights burning, wait, open. The lord comes, knocks, finds them watching, girds himself and serves.',
    '**Between settings.** Matthew and Mark stand on the mount of Olives after a question about “these things.” Luke 12 stands in teaching to disciples with a multitude gathered, with no such question.',
    '**Between these sayings and their neighbors.** The thief belongs to the sayings of the goodman only. The porter belongs to Mark only. The lord who serves belongs to Luke only. The lamps and the bridegroom belong to the Ten Virgins, and the faithful and wise servant belongs to his own saying.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the thief, the house, the servants, the porter, the lord, the wedding, the loins or the lights. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Ten Virgins, the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant, the Fig Tree Lesson, the Talents or the Sheep and the Goats, and no meaning carried between the three accounts.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Watching, waiting, opening and being found sleeping are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
    '**What must not be said of the time.** The hour, the watch and the coming are not dated or put in order with other events.',
]))

