A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Ten Virgins, the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant, the Fig Tree Lesson and the Sheep and the Goats are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. These sayings make no use of their meanings, and they make none of these.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus says that a goodman who had known the watch or the hour of the thief would have watched and would not have suffered his house to be broken up or broken through, and He says to be ready, for the Son of man cometh at an hour not thought. He says the Son of man is as a man taking a far journey, who gave authority to his servants and to every man his work and commanded the porter to watch, and that no one knows when the master of the house cometh, and that what He says to them He says to all: Watch. He tells His hearers to keep loins girded and lights burning, like men who wait for their lord, and He blesses the servants whom the lord finds watching, whom he shall gird himself and serve. He gives no explanation of the thief, the house, the servants, the porter or the lord. The comparison says that watching and readiness are called for because the hour is not known.'))
